WHIPFX's fork of oxmysql (github.com/WHIPFX/oxmysql) has this fix in its source, and its releases need no
patching; WHIP's txAdmin recipe installs it. This file is for putting the fix into a stock oxmysql build by hand.

whip's patch to oxmysql (P-001, 2 Oct 2026). Not loaded by fxmanifest.lua.

When oxmysql cannot get a connection (the database is down or restarting) the error from
`await using connection = await getConnection()` escapes its query, execute, transaction and
startTransaction functions as a rejected promise nobody awaits, so the caller is never answered: a
Lua MySQL.*.await waits for ever and Node prints an unhandled rejection. Every other failure there
answers through logError; this one was missed. Seen in Josh's outage test on 2 Oct 2026; upstream
master (src/database/rawQuery.ts, connection.ts) had the same code that day.

The patch answers that case as an error, the way oxmysql already answers a bad query. Safe: the
query never reached the database. Whip's db_guard.lua (65 s) stays as the backstop, and whip_core
says at start-up when this patch is missing (an oxmysql update replaces dist/build.js).

To re-apply after an update: make the same four edits in [deps]/oxmysql/dist/build.js — wrap each
`await getConnection(...)` in rawQuery, rawExecute, rawTransaction and startTransaction in a
try/catch that returns logError(...), keeping the marker comment. Line numbers move between
versions; the code around them does not.

--- oxmysql 2.12.0 dist/build.js (upstream)
+++ oxmysql 2.12.0 dist/build.js (whip P-001 patch)
@@ -26027,7 +26027,13 @@
     } catch (err) {
       return logError(invokingResource, cb, isPromise, err, query, parameters);
     }
-    const connection = __using(_stack, await getConnection(connectionId));
+    let connection;
+    try {
+      connection = __using(_stack, await getConnection(connectionId));
+    } catch (err) {
+      // whip P-001 patch: no connection answers as an error. Upstream lets it escape to a promise nobody awaits, so the caller is never answered.
+      return logError(invokingResource, cb, isPromise, err, query, parameters);
+    }
     if (!connection) return;
     try {
       const hasProfiler = await runProfiler(connection, invokingResource);
